{%- comment -%}
  Rating line computed from the same review metaobjects the reviews section
  reads. Renders nothing at all when there is nothing to show, so it costs a
  card no space until real reviews exist.

  Usage:
    {% render 'product-rating', product: product %}                  <- a card
    {% render 'product-rating', scope: 'store', verbose: true %}     <- a grid head

  Params:
    product   required for the default scope
    scope     'product' (default) counts only reviews linked to this product,
              and renders nothing when the product has none of its own.
              'store' counts every review in the metaobject, whatever it is
              linked to, and ignores `product`.
    verbose   true writes "12 reviews" instead of "(12)" (default false)
    class     extra class on the wrapper (default none)

  Why the store-level number is no longer a per-product fallback:
    This snippet used to fall back to unlinked, store-level reviews whenever a
    product had none of its own. With one product that was defensible; with a
    grid it meant every card printed the identical star row and identical count,
    which is the exact visual signature of invented social proof. A shopper who
    reads two cards and sees the same number twice discounts all of it. So the
    store-level figure is still shown — once, on the grid heading, where it is
    true — and a card speaks only for its own product or says nothing.

  Never invents a rating: with zero reviews in scope the whole block is absent,
  not a row of empty stars implying a score of nothing.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- liquid
  assign want = scope | default: 'product'
  assign sum = 0
  assign n = 0

  for r in metaobjects.review.values
    if want == 'store'
      assign sum = sum | plus: r.rating.value
      assign n = n | plus: 1
    elsif r.product.value != blank and r.product.value.id == product.id
      assign sum = sum | plus: r.rating.value
      assign n = n | plus: 1
    endif
  endfor
-%}
{%- if n > 0 -%}
  {%- liquid
    assign avg = sum | times: 1.0 | divided_by: n | round: 1
    comment
      The fill width is derived from the same rounded average that is printed
      beside it, so the stars and the numeral can never disagree. The old
      version rounded to whole stars, which drew 4.6 as five solid stars — it
      overstated the score, contradicted its own aria-label, and threw away the
      more persuasive number of the two.
    endcomment
    assign pct = avg | times: 20
  -%}
  <p class="pc-rating {{ class }}">
    <span class="pc-rating-stars" role="img" aria-label="Rated {{ avg }} out of 5 from {{ n }} review{% if n != 1 %}s{% endif %}">
      <span class="pc-rating-track" aria-hidden="true">★★★★★</span>
      <span class="pc-rating-fill" aria-hidden="true" style="width:{{ pct }}%">★★★★★</span>
    </span>
    <span class="pc-rating-avg" aria-hidden="true">{{ avg }}</span>
    <span class="pc-rating-count" aria-hidden="true">
      {%- if verbose -%}{{ n }} review{% if n != 1 %}s{% endif %}{%- else -%}({{ n }}){%- endif -%}
    </span>
  </p>
{%- endif -%}
